    "verdict": "ACCEPT finite source addendum; runtime HOLD",
    "text": "# Independent review: tooltip inspection input producer\n\n**ACCEPT the finite source addendum. Runtime implementation remains HOLD.**\n\nReviewed `input-producer-addendum.md` SHA256 `670d0c22cca725ee3c86b0066753d432b505bcb26aba498972892cb92f3c9b20` and `receipt.json` SHA256 `56b089558e971732bdaf071ef576726b46adb7f08b5f7d27b6533449e56c5745` in `/workspace/scratch/69fd8163d94e/issue19-tooltip-controller-source-20261009/work/orchestration/tooltip-input-producer-20261009-1225/`. Research head remains clean `dee602609ff99a9201c71b0694a3bd0784a88867`; comparison main is `47680b8fd124aab50b0528eea77a81cacb556060`.\n\nIndependently verified all 22 artifact hashes and 14 source hashes, including the four current application sources at explicit main. Checked eight retained data ranges, all seven 16-byte mouse object records and referenced axis GUIDs against the verified PE's section mapping. Also matched all 1,337 retained disassembly byte lines against the unchanged inert EXE SHA256 `3a5065c7420b3fcde208bf220bc86dfbac95e025ab2492caf9c7ea5308dfbe4f`. This byte check did not execute or disassemble original instructions.\n\nThe actual call at `0052cdfb` uses descriptor `00584fb0`, not an assumed default layout. Its header is `{24,16,2,16,7,00584f40}`; the descriptor at `00584f80` requests a button at byte 13 with type `00ffff0c` and a null GUID. The [Microsoft SDK header](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/microsoft/win32metadata/main/generation/WinSDK/RecompiledIdlHeaders/um/dinput.h) confirms the system-mouse GUID, flags and vtable positions: GetDeviceData at +0x28 and SetDataFormat at +0x2c. The [DIDATAFORMAT](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/desktop/ee416606(v=vs.85)) and [DIOBJECTDATAFORMAT](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/desktop/ee416633(v=vs.85)) documentation support the field interpretation. `ANYINSTANCE` remains significant; the [mouse documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/desktop/ee418262(v=vs.85)) describes the second traditional mouse button as generally right and the high bit as pressed state. The addendum correctly qualifies this as the normal configured second-button route, rather than a universal physical-switch or remapping guarantee. SetDataFormat's HRESULT is not checked locally, and this assessment does not assert successful device operation at runtime.\n\nOn the ordinary successful producer/queue path, offset 13 reaches the button branch, `(dwData & 0x80) >> 7` supplies polarity, and `0052d049` stores F1. The 10-byte enqueue/dequeue copies preserve code/state; raw event type 2 becomes callback event type 1. The F1 jump-table entry reaches the mouse callback slot, whose retained table supplies `004aa1c0`. That consumer maps nonzero/zero to resolver events 1/4, subject to its earlier interceptors. The numeric records then resolve to cases 0x72/0x73. Queue availability, device failure and lock failure are not claims of guaranteed delivery. The finite chain closes the previously missing ordinary producer correspondence.\n\nThe direct configuration lifecycle is also supported: `00451330`/`00451350` set/clear bit 0x100 of the 30-byte indexed record; record 2 is at word `005a21d8`. `0045db60` calls `0045ed10`, sets this bit, saves the prior mode and enters 0x11 via `0047a550`; its clear path restores through the same helper. Existing cases 0xb9/0xba reach that owner with their documented conditions. The selected-person count relation in `0047a550`/`00418d70` is accurate but does not prove equivalence for every mode writer or every browser selected ID.\n\nThe current browser down/up, modal, selection and release-cancellation differences are accurately retained as unresolved comparisons. No reduction of native level flags, valid-cell state or configuration ownership to `inputMask`, overview or empty selection is accepted. The prior shared tooltip-cache side effects and fresh-versus-reused inspection record distinction remain controlling.\n\nThe exact next bounded guard question is `0045db60`'s immediate `0045ed10` UI owner and cases 0xb9/0xba, compared with the browser's modal/pause/overview lifecycle. Other named level, valid-cell and mode-writer gaps remain explicit; this verdict does not authorize expanding into them or a generalized input rewrite. No further correction is required for this finite source addendum.\n\nReview activity was limited to retained file/git reads, hash and inert-byte comparison, and official documentation reads. No application edits, dependencies, tests, original execution/emulation, Ghidra, browser application run or new disassembly occurred.\n"
  },
